{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 5, :comments ["<b>Glory over me, etc.</b> One needs to know the intent of his saying <b>glory over me</b>, etc. And if one were to say that although he tells him to remove it from upon him immediately, this itself is the glorifying — if so, when Pharaoh said to him ״tomorrow,״ and did not say now at this time, which would indicate that he did not wish to glory over him, what room is there to say to him ״according to your word, in order that you may know״? This is no glorifying from one day to the next; on the contrary, he delayed the time for him. Further, one must examine the words of Moses, who said <b>For when shall I entreat</b>, etc. — he should have said nothing other than: when will He remove the frogs, which is what is sought." "<b>Indeed,</b> Moses' intent was to show him that HaShem performs wonders to endear His beloved, and from this he would estimate and conclude in his mind that it is impossible for HaShem to abandon them in any manner whatsoever. For behold, it is the way of ethics: when a slave asks something of his master, and would that he attain it — they do not impose upon him a fixed time, so long as he does not make it too early and so long as he does not make it too late; rather, a slave longs for his master to choose an hour when he desires to show him favor. And Moses said: come and see the precise providence and the perfection of endearment — that I will entreat HaShem and say to Him in my prayer that He do the thing at the time that I fix for Him. And this is his saying <b>For when shall I entreat</b> — meaning: for what time do you glory over me, saying: pray to HaShem now, for at such-and-such a time He will remove, etc."], :book :exodus, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 8, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:25:51.687695548Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}